# Part C Outline: Specialized Technology

## C1 Concepts
- What computer vision is: computers understanding images; moved from hand-written rules to deep learning
- Three common tasks: classification, object detection, segmentation
  - This project uses classification (cardiomegaly vs normal) and segmentation (heart and lungs for CTR)
- Key concepts
  1. An image is a grid of numbers (0 = black, 255 = white); preprocessing: resize, normalise, CLAHE
  2. CNN: kernels slide over the image; early layers find edges, deeper layers find shapes such as the heart border
  3. Vision Transformer: image cut into patches; self-attention compares every patch with the others
  4. Supervised learning: loss function and backpropagation update the weights
  5. Output: sigmoid gives a probability; temperature scaling calibrates it; Grad-CAM shows a heatmap
- Figure 1: flowchart of the screening system
- Worked examples
  - Convolution: vertical-edge kernel on a lung–heart border gives 570 (edge) vs 0 (no edge)
  - Calibration: score 1.2 → probability 0.77 → after T = 1.5, 0.69 → below 0.70 threshold → sent for review
  - CTR: 520 ÷ 940 ≈ 0.55 > 0.50 → enlarged heart

## C2 Frameworks, models, methods and tools
- Models
  - ResNet-50: CNN with skip connections; main classifier
  - Vision Transformer (ViT): second classifier, compared with ResNet-50
  - U-Net: encoder–decoder that segments heart and lungs to measure CTR
- Methods
  - CLAHE: improves local contrast
  - Grad-CAM: heatmap of regions that drove the prediction
  - Temperature scaling: calibrates confidence using the validation set
- Framework and tool
  - PyTorch: build, train and test models on GPU
  - OpenCV: load and prepare images, apply CLAHE, overlay heatmaps

## C3 Fit to the problem
- How it helps
  - Same result for the same image: less subjective, fewer missed mild cases
  - U-Net measures CTR automatically
  - Calibrated confidence and Grad-CAM help clinicians decide when to trust a result
  - External testing on VinDr-CXR checks performance on unseen data
- Benefits: fast and consistent screening, several types of evidence, uncertain cases sent to a professional
- Limitations and risks
  - NIH labels taken automatically from reports, so some are wrong
  - Lower performance on external data and under-represented groups
  - A good-looking heatmap does not prove the prediction is correct
  - Patient X-rays are sensitive: consent, anonymisation, secure storage
- Alternative: rule-based CTR only (flag if CTR > 0.50)
  - Simple, easy to understand, no cardiomegaly labels needed
  - But depends on accurate borders, affected by scoliosis and consolidation, cannot show uncertainty
- Choice: deep learning classifier as the main approach, CTR kept as a cross-check
